¶
'''wareless†''' (wãr′les), ''a.'' [< ''ware''<sup>1</sup> + ''-less''.] '''1.''' Unwary; incautious; heedless.

{{smaller block|A bait the ''wareless'' to beguile.
{{right|''Mir. for Mags.'' (''Latham.'')}}}}
'''2.''' Unaware; regardless.

{{smaller block|Both they unwise, and ''warelesse'' of the evill.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. ii. 3.}}}}
'''3.''' Unperceived.

{{smaller block|<poem>When he wak’t out of his ''wareless'' paine, . . .
That lim he could not wag. ''Spenser'', F. Q., V. i. 22.</poem>}}

'''warely†''' (wãr′li), ''a.'' [< ME. ''warly'', ''warliche'', < AS. ''wærlīc'', cautious, < ''wær'', cautious, + ''-līc'' = E. ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>.] Cautious; prudent; wary.

{{smaller block|<poem>The Petyuins tham bare as ''warly'' men fre;
For ther good vitail and wines plente.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1362.}}}}

'''warely†''' (wãr′li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''warly'', ''werly'', ''warliche'', < AS. ''wærlīce'', < ''wær'', cautious, + ''-līce'' = E. ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>. Cf. ''warily''.] Cautiously; warily.

{{smaller block|Full ''warly'' in this nede. ''Chaucer'', Troilus, iii. 454.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Bi hys huge prowesse went it to assaill
In ryght ''werly'' wyse, for manly was in breste.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1591.}}}}
{{smaller block|A good lesson to use our tongue ''warely'', that our wordes and matter maie . . . agree together.
{{right|''Sir T. Wilson'', Art of Rhetoric (ed. 1584), p. 168.}}}}

'''wareroom''' (wãr′röm), ''n.'' A room in which goods are stored or laid out for sale.

{{smaller block|Philip was still in the ''wareroom'', arranging goods and taking stock. ''Mrs. Gaskell'', Sylvia’s Lovers, xxxii.}}

'''war-fain''' (wâr′fān), ''a.'' Eager to fight. [Poetical.]
